// x509path — path validation as a pure function.
//
// WHAT PATH VALIDATION IS.  A certificate on its own says nothing.  It
// becomes meaningful when there is a chain from it to something the
// reader already trusts: certificate 0 was issued by certificate 1,
// which was issued by certificate 2, which was issued by a TRUST
// ANCHOR the reader was configured with.  RFC 5280 section 6 states
// the algorithm: walk the chain from the anchor down, and at every
// step check the signature, the validity period, the names, the basic
// constraints, the key usage and the name constraints.
//
// AND HERE IT IS A PURE FUNCTION.  `validate` reads no clock, opens no
// file and makes no network call.  Everything arrives as an argument:
//
//   the chain        — certificates the caller parsed
//   the trust set    — anchors the caller built, from wherever it
//                      keeps them
//   the verifier     — the caller's signature check, or none
//   the instant      — the moment the caller read from its own clock
//   the policy       — what the caller's rules are, as ONE value
//
// Three by-products are worth naming, because they are what the shape
// is for.  A test validates a chain at any instant with no fake clock
// and no injected time source.  An audit tool asks whether a
// certificate was valid when it was PRESENTED — six weeks ago, in a
// request log — rather than only whether it is valid now.  And a
// replay of that log gives exactly the answers it gave the first time,
// because there is nothing in the function for the second run to
// disagree with.  It is the same shape jwt-nv's `JwtInstant` takes for
// the same reason.
//
// THE POLICY IS A VALUE, NOT A PILE OF OPTIONAL ARGUMENTS.  Every
// setting below is a decision somebody has to make about what their
// program accepts, and settings that live in a call's arguments drift
// between call sites until nobody can say what the program accepts.  A
// service builds one policy, names it, and hands the same one
// everywhere — so "what do we accept" has a definition, a place in the
// source and a diff when it changes.  jwt-nv's `JwtPolicy` is the
// model, `strict()` is deliberately awkward, and narrowing it is the
// one moment somebody looks.
//
// THE REFUSAL NAMES WHICH CERTIFICATE AND WHY.  "The chain is invalid"
// is not an answer anybody can act on.  Every rule failure carries the
// index of the certificate in the chain — 0 for the end-entity
// certificate, counting up towards the anchor — and `x509err.code`
// gives the reason as a stable token.
//
// ONE BUFFER HOLDS THE CHAIN.  Every certificate in `chain` carries
// spans into `src`, so they must all have been parsed out of the same
// bytes — which is what `x509cert.parse_all` produces and what a TLS
// Certificate message is.  An ANCHOR is different: it arrived on its
// own, from a trust store, so `X509Anchor` carries its own bytes.

use std.bytes
use asn1oid
use asn1read
use x509cert
use x509ext
use x509name
use x509verify

// The chain length `strict()` bounds a path at.
//
// Eight certificates below the anchor, which is more than any chain in
// public use and less than a graph somebody built to be walked
// forever.  A bound of this package's choosing and not a rule of RFC
// 5280, which is why `with_max_chain_length` exists.
pub const X509_MAX_CHAIN_DEFAULT = 8

// A trust anchor: a certificate the caller has decided to believe
// without checking anything above it.
//
// It carries its OWN bytes.  An anchor comes from a trust store, a
// configuration file or a device's provisioning, so its spans point
// into those bytes rather than into the chain being validated.
pub struct X509Anchor
    // The anchor's DER, whole.  Every span in `cert` points into this.
    der: Bytes
    // The anchor's certificate, parsed.  A whole certificate rather
    // than a name and a key, because an anchor may carry name
    // constraints that apply to everything below it, and a validation
    // that ignored them would ignore the main reason a private CA is
    // constrained at all.
    cert: X509Cert

// The anchors a validation may terminate at.
//
// A value the caller builds: this package never reads a trust store,
// because reading one is `[io]` and because whose certificates a
// program believes is the most important configuration it has and
// should be visible in its source.
pub struct X509TrustSet
    // The anchors, in no particular order.  A validation tries every
    // one whose subject name matches.
    anchors: [X509Anchor]

// What a validation checks, as one value.
//
// Built from `strict()` and narrowed, or — rarely and visibly — from
// `permissive()`.  Carried by value: two services in one program hold
// different policies and neither can change the other's.
pub struct X509PathPolicy
    // The most certificates that may sit below the anchor.
    // `X509_MAX_CHAIN_DEFAULT` in `strict()`.
    max_chain_length: Int
    // Whether every certificate that signed another must carry
    // `basicConstraints` with the CA flag set.  `true` in `strict()`,
    // and RFC 5280 section 6.1.4 requires it.
    require_ca_flag: Bool
    // Whether every certificate that signed another must carry
    // `keyUsage` with `keyCertSign`.  `true` in `strict()`.  A CA
    // whose key usage forbids signing certificates has signed nothing
    // anybody should accept.
    require_key_cert_sign: Bool
    // Whether `basicConstraints.pathLenConstraint` is enforced.
    // `true` in `strict()`.
    enforce_path_len: Bool
    // Whether `nameConstraints` extensions are applied to every
    // certificate below the one that carries them.  `true` in
    // `strict()`.
    //
    // Turning it off is the one setting that is nearly always wrong: a
    // name constraint is a CRITICAL extension, so a reader that does
    // not apply it is required by RFC 5280 section 4.2 to refuse the
    // certificate instead.  `without_name_constraints` therefore also
    // has to declare the OID known, and says so.
    enforce_name_constraints: Bool
    // Whether every certificate's validity period is checked against
    // the instant.  `true` in `strict()`.  `false` is for a tool
    // inspecting an expired chain on purpose.
    check_validity: Bool
    // Whether a signature no verifier could check is a refusal.
    // `true` in `strict()`.
    //
    // `false` is what an inventory tool passes: the path validates,
    // and every index it could not check is in `X509Path.unchecked`
    // for the caller to read.  It is the setting that makes
    // `x509verify.no_verifier` useful rather than merely possible.
    require_all_signatures_checked: Bool
    // Extension OIDs the CALLER implements, beyond the ones this
    // package reads.  A critical extension named here is not unknown.
    // Empty in `strict()`.
    known_extension_oids: [AsnOid]
    // An extended key usage the end-entity certificate must carry, or
    // `None` for no requirement.  `None` in `strict()`, because the
    // purpose depends on what the program is doing and this package
    // cannot guess.
    required_key_purpose: ?AsnOid

// The validated path.
//
// What `validate` answers on success, and it carries more than "yes":
// a caller usually needs to know what it got, not only that it got
// something.
pub struct X509Path
    // The chain as validated, the end-entity certificate first and the
    // certificate the anchor issued last.  The anchor itself is not in
    // this list; `anchor` is.
    certificates: [X509Cert]
    // The anchor the path terminated at.  Which anchor it was matters:
    // a program with an internal CA and a public one wants to know
    // which of them vouched for a peer.
    anchor: X509Anchor
    // The index of every certificate whose signature no verifier could
    // check, in chain order.  **Empty when every signature was
    // checked**, and non-empty only under a policy that allows it.
    //
    // A caller that reports "valid" without reading this is claiming
    // something it was told it could not claim.
    unchecked: [Int]
    // Every policy OID asserted by every certificate on the path.  The
    // policy PROCESSING of RFC 5280 section 6.1 — the valid-policy
    // tree, mapping, the inhibit counters — is not implemented, and
    // this is what is offered instead: the facts, for a caller that
    // has a rule about them.
    policies: [AsnOid]
    // How many certificates sit below the anchor.  The length
    // `max_chain_length` bounds.
    length: Int

// ── the trust set ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// An anchor from a certificate's DER.
//
// The bytes are kept inside the anchor, so a caller may drop its own
// copy.  `X509NotDer` when the bytes are not a certificate.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use asn1read
// use x509path
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let der = bytes.zeros(0)
//     match x509path.anchor(der, asn1read.default_limits())
//         Ok(a)  => println("${a.cert.version}")
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn anchor(der: Bytes, limits: AsnLimits) -> Result<X509Anchor, X509Error> []
    let cert = x509cert.parse(der, limits)!
    Ok(X509Anchor { der: bytes.clone(der), cert: cert })

// A trust set from anchors the caller built.
pub fn trust_set(anchors: [X509Anchor]) -> X509TrustSet []
    X509TrustSet { anchors: list.clone(anchors) }

// A trust set with no anchors in it.
//
// Every validation against it fails with `X509EmptyTrustSet`, which is
// a configuration mistake with its own name rather than a chain that
// mysteriously would not terminate.
pub fn empty_trust_set() -> X509TrustSet []
    X509TrustSet { anchors: [] }

// The anchors, in the order they were given.
pub fn anchors_of(t: X509TrustSet) -> [X509Anchor] []
    t.anchors[:]

// How many anchors the set holds.
pub fn anchor_count(t: X509TrustSet) -> Int []
    list.len(t.anchors)

// A trust set with one more anchor.
//
// Answers a new set; the old one is unchanged, so a program that
// builds a base set and two variants of it holds three independent
// values.
pub fn with_anchor(t: X509TrustSet, a: X509Anchor) -> X509TrustSet []
    var out = t.anchors[:]
    list.push(out, a)
    X509TrustSet { anchors: out }

// The first anchor whose subject name encoding equals this issuer
// name, or `None`.
//
// The comparison is over the DER, which is why the issuer arrives with
// the bytes it was read from.
pub fn find_anchor(t: X509TrustSet, src: Bytes, issuer: AsnName) -> ?X509Anchor []
    for a in t.anchors
        if x509name.issuer_matches_subject(src, issuer, a.der,
                                           x509cert.subject_of(a.cert))
            return Some(a)
    None

// ── the policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The policy that checks everything.
//
// A chain bounded at `X509_MAX_CHAIN_DEFAULT`, a CA flag required, a
// `keyCertSign` bit required, path lengths enforced, name constraints
// enforced, validity checked against the instant, every signature
// required to have been checked, no extension known beyond this
// package's own, and no key purpose required.
//
// It is the starting point, and narrowing it is deliberate work: each
// call below turns one check off or adds one requirement, and the diff
// says which.
//
// ```novo
// use x509path
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${x509path.strict().require_ca_flag}")   // true
// ```
pub fn strict() -> X509PathPolicy []
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: X509_MAX_CHAIN_DEFAULT, require_ca_flag: true,
                     require_key_cert_sign: true, enforce_path_len: true,
                     enforce_name_constraints: true, check_validity: true,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: true, known_extension_oids: [],
                     required_key_purpose: None }

// **A policy that checks the shape of a chain and almost nothing
// else.**
//
// No CA flag, no key usage, no path lengths, no name constraints, no
// validity, and unchecked signatures allowed.  The chain must still
// reach an anchor and the names must still match, because a "path"
// that did not would not be a path.
//
// It is for a certificate viewer, a linter or a migration reading
// chains whose anchors expired years ago.  It is not for anything that
// decides whether to trust a peer, and the name is what it is so that
// a reviewer meeting it in a request path asks why.
pub fn permissive() -> X509PathPolicy []
    // It does not enforce name constraints and it therefore also
    // declares the OID known: leaving a nameConstraints extension
    // unknown would refuse every constrained chain, which is the one
    // thing a policy that "checks the shape and almost nothing else"
    // must not do.
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: X509_MAX_CHAIN_DEFAULT, require_ca_flag: false,
                     require_key_cert_sign: false, enforce_path_len: false,
                     enforce_name_constraints: false, check_validity: false,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: false,
                     known_extension_oids: [x509ext.oid_name_constraints()],
                     required_key_purpose: None }

// The same policy with a different bound on the chain length.
//
// A bound below 1 makes the policy unusable, which `check` reports.
//
// ```novo
// use x509path
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = x509path.with_max_chain_length(x509path.strict(), 3)
//     println("${p.max_chain_length}")   // 3
// ```
pub fn with_max_chain_length(p: X509PathPolicy, n: Int) -> X509PathPolicy []
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: n, require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: p.enforce_name_constraints,
                     check_validity: p.check_validity,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: p.require_all_signatures_checked,
                     known_extension_oids: p.known_extension_oids[:],
                     required_key_purpose: p.required_key_purpose }

// The same policy, admitting signatures no verifier could check.
//
// What an inventory tool passes with `x509verify.no_verifier`.  The
// indices land in `X509Path.unchecked`, and a caller that does not
// read them is claiming more than it was told.
pub fn allowing_unchecked_signatures(p: X509PathPolicy) -> X509PathPolicy []
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: p.max_chain_length,
                     require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: p.enforce_name_constraints,
                     check_validity: p.check_validity, require_all_signatures_checked: false,
                     known_extension_oids: p.known_extension_oids[:],
                     required_key_purpose: p.required_key_purpose }

// The same policy, with these extension OIDs counted as known.
//
// A program that implements an extension of its own names it here, and
// a critical one stops being a reason to refuse.  It is the only way
// to widen what "recognized" means in RFC 5280 section 4.2, and it is
// a per-program decision rather than a per-package one.
pub fn knowing_extensions(p: X509PathPolicy, oids: [AsnOid]) -> X509PathPolicy []
    var known = p.known_extension_oids[:]
    for o in oids
        list.push(known, o)
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: p.max_chain_length,
                     require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: p.enforce_name_constraints,
                     check_validity: p.check_validity,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: p.require_all_signatures_checked,
                     known_extension_oids: known, required_key_purpose: p.required_key_purpose }

// The same policy, requiring the end-entity certificate to carry this
// extended key usage.
//
// `x509ext.oid_kp_server_auth` for a TLS server, `oid_kp_client_auth`
// for a client.  A certificate with no `extKeyUsage` at all carries no
// restriction and passes; one that names purposes and not this one is
// refused with `X509KeyPurposeNotPermitted`.
pub fn requiring_key_purpose(p: X509PathPolicy, purpose: AsnOid) -> X509PathPolicy []
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: p.max_chain_length,
                     require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: p.enforce_name_constraints,
                     check_validity: p.check_validity,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: p.require_all_signatures_checked,
                     known_extension_oids: p.known_extension_oids[:],
                     required_key_purpose: Some(purpose) }

// The same policy, not checking validity periods.
//
// For a tool inspecting an expired chain on purpose.  It does not make
// an expired certificate valid; it makes the validation stop asking.
pub fn without_validity_check(p: X509PathPolicy) -> X509PathPolicy []
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: p.max_chain_length,
                     require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: p.enforce_name_constraints,
                     check_validity: false,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: p.require_all_signatures_checked,
                     known_extension_oids: p.known_extension_oids[:],
                     required_key_purpose: p.required_key_purpose }

// The same policy, not enforcing name constraints — and counting the
// `nameConstraints` OID as known so the chain is not then refused for
// carrying a critical extension nobody processed.
//
// The two go together and are therefore one call.  A policy that
// turned the check off and left the OID unknown would refuse every
// constrained chain, which is not what anybody reaching for this
// wants, and a policy that turned it off and silently ignored the
// extension would accept a certificate restricted in a way it never
// read.  The name says what it costs.
pub fn without_name_constraints(p: X509PathPolicy) -> X509PathPolicy []
    var known = p.known_extension_oids[:]
    list.push(known, x509ext.oid_name_constraints())
    X509PathPolicy { max_chain_length: p.max_chain_length,
                     require_ca_flag: p.require_ca_flag,
                     require_key_cert_sign: p.require_key_cert_sign,
                     enforce_path_len: p.enforce_path_len,
                     enforce_name_constraints: false,
                     check_validity: p.check_validity,
                     require_all_signatures_checked: p.require_all_signatures_checked,
                     known_extension_oids: known, required_key_purpose: p.required_key_purpose }

// Whether the policy could ever accept anything, and why not.
//
// `Some(X509PolicyUnusable)` for a maximum chain length below 1.
// `None` when it is usable.
//
// It is here because that is a configuration mistake rather than a
// certificate problem, and finding it at start-up — where the message
// can name the field — is worth much more than finding it per request
// as a stream of rejections that all look like somebody else's fault.
pub fn check_policy(p: X509PathPolicy) -> ?X509Error []
    if p.max_chain_length < 1
        return Some(X509PolicyUnusable(why: "a maximum chain length below one can never reach an anchor"))
    None

// ── the parts the walk below shares ────────────────────────────────

// The outcome of one link of a chain: nothing wrong, a refusal, or a
// signature nobody could check.
//
// Three answers rather than a `Result`, because "unchecked" is a
// SUCCESS under a policy that admits it and the index still has to
// reach the caller.  A `Result` would have to spell it as an `Ok` with
// a flag anyway, and the flag is the whole point.
struct PathLink
    // The refusal, when the link failed.
    fault: ?X509Error
    // True when the verifier had no implementation of the algorithm
    // and the policy admitted that.
    unchecked: Bool

// Whether the pool already holds this certificate.
//
// By the span it occupies rather than by value: every certificate in
// one pool was parsed out of one buffer, so two that point at the same
// bytes are the same certificate and two that do not are not.
fn holds_cert(pool: [X509Cert], c: X509Cert) -> Bool []
    for other in pool
        if other.der.off == c.der.off and other.der.len == c.der.len
            return true
    false

// Every nameConstraints extension on the certificates above `index`,
// the anchor's included.
fn constraints_above(src: Bytes, chain: [X509Cert], index: Int, anchor: X509Anchor,
                     limits: AsnLimits) -> Result<[X509NameConstraints], X509Error> []
    var out: [X509NameConstraints] = []
    for i in index + 1..list.len(chain)
        let here = x509ext.name_constraints(chain[i].extensions, limits)!
        if let Some(c) = here
            list.push(out, c)
    let at_anchor = x509ext.name_constraints(anchor.cert.extensions, limits)!
    if let Some(c) = at_anchor
        list.push(out, c)
    Ok(out)

// Every name a certificate asserts: its subject, written as a
// directory name, and everything in its subjectAltName.
fn asserted_names(c: X509Cert, limits: AsnLimits) -> Result<[X509GeneralName], X509Error> []
    var out: [X509GeneralName] = [X509DirectoryName(name: c.subject)]
    let alt = x509ext.subject_alt_name(c.extensions, limits)!
    if let Some(names) = alt
        for n in names
            list.push(out, n)
    Ok(out)

// The first name of `names` that some constraint refuses, or `None`
// when every one of them is permitted.
//
// `names_permitted` is this answering `None`, and `validate` wants the
// name itself so that the refusal can print it — "a name constraint
// was violated" without saying which name is not an answer anybody can
// act on.
fn first_refused(constraints: [X509NameConstraints],
                 names: [X509GeneralName]) -> ?X509GeneralName []
    for n in names
        for c in constraints
            if not x509ext.constraints_permit(c, n)
                return Some(n)
    None

// One link of the chain: the names must match, the issuer's own rules
// must permit it to have signed something, and the signature must
// check — or be one no verifier could check.
//
// `anchored` is true when the issuer is the TRUST ANCHOR.  An anchor is
// believed rather than checked, so its basic constraints, its key usage
// and its own path length are not asked: an anchor is where the walk
// stops, and a reader that demanded a CA flag of it would refuse every
// trust store that holds a bare name-and-key.
//
// `below` is the number of non-self-issued intermediate certificates
// between the end-entity certificate and the issuer, which is what a
// pathLenConstraint bounds (RFC 5280 section 4.2.1.9 and 6.1.4 (l) and
// (m)).  The end-entity certificate is not an intermediate and a
// self-issued one is not counted, so a CA with pathLenConstraint 0 may
// issue the end-entity certificate itself.
fn link(src: Bytes, c: X509Cert, issuer_src: Bytes, issuer: X509Cert, index: Int, below: Int,
        verifier: X509Verifier, policy: X509PathPolicy, limits: AsnLimits,
        anchored: Bool) -> PathLink []
    let here = x509cert.issuer_of(c)
    if not x509name.issuer_matches_subject(src, here, issuer_src,
                                           x509cert.subject_of(issuer))
        return PathLink { fault: Some(X509IssuerNotFound(index: index,
                          issuer: x509name.to_rfc4514(here))),
                          unchecked: false }
    let exts = x509cert.extensions_of(issuer)
    if not anchored
        if policy.require_ca_flag
            match x509ext.is_ca(exts, limits)
                Err(e) =>
                    return PathLink { fault: Some(e), unchecked: false }
                Ok(ca) =>
                    if not ca
                        return PathLink {
                            fault: Some(X509NotACertificateAuthority(index: index + 1)),
                            unchecked: false }
        if policy.require_key_cert_sign
            match x509ext.key_usage(exts, limits)
                Err(e)    =>
                    return PathLink { fault: Some(e), unchecked: false }
                Ok(usage) =>
                    if let Some(k) = usage
                        if not k.key_cert_sign
                            return PathLink {
                                fault: Some(X509KeyCertSignNotPermitted(index: index + 1)),
                                unchecked: false }
        if policy.enforce_path_len
            match x509ext.basic_constraints(exts, limits)
                Err(e)          =>
                    return PathLink { fault: Some(e), unchecked: false }
                Ok(constraints) =>
                    if let Some(b) = constraints
                        if b.path_len >= 0 and below > b.path_len
                            return PathLink {
                                fault: Some(X509PathLenExceeded(index: index + 1,
                                                                limit: b.path_len)),
                                unchecked: false }
    match x509verify.verify_certificate(verifier, x509cert.public_key_of(issuer), src, c)
        Err(e)      =>
            return PathLink { fault: Some(e), unchecked: false }
        Ok(outcome) =>
            if x509verify.outcome_is_good(outcome)
                return PathLink { fault: None, unchecked: false }
            if x509verify.outcome_is_uncheckable(outcome)
                if policy.require_all_signatures_checked
                    return PathLink {
                        fault: Some(X509NoVerifierForAlgorithm(index: index,
                            algorithm: x509verify.algorithm_name(c.outer_algorithm))),
                        unchecked: false }
                return PathLink { fault: None, unchecked: true }
            PathLink { fault: Some(X509SignatureInvalid(index: index)), unchecked: false }

// Whether a DNS name matches a certificate's pattern.
//
// The wildcard rule of RFC 6125 section 6.4.3: `*` matches exactly one
// label and only in the leftmost position, so `*.example.com` matches
// `a.example.com` and neither `example.com` nor `a.b.example.com`.
//
// Both sides arrive lower-cased, because DNS is case-insensitive and a
// certificate written `*.EXAMPLE.COM` is the same certificate.
fn dns_matches(pattern: Str, host: Str) -> Bool []
    if str.len(pattern) > 2 and str.starts_with(pattern, "*.")
        let rest = str.slice(pattern, 2, str.len(pattern))
        let suffix = "." + rest
        if str.len(host) <= str.len(suffix)
            return false
        if not str.ends_with(host, suffix)
            return false
        let label = str.slice(host, 0, str.len(host) - str.len(suffix))
        return not str.contains(label, ".")
    pattern == host

// ── validation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// **Validate a chain against a trust set, at an instant, under a
// policy.**
//
// The function this package exists for, and a pure one: no clock, no
// file, no network.  `src` is the buffer every certificate in `chain`
// was parsed out of; `chain` is ordered with the end-entity
// certificate first; `at` is the moment the caller read from its own
// clock; `verifier` is the caller's signature check, possibly
// `x509verify.no_verifier`.
//
// On success the path says which anchor it reached, how long it was,
// which policies were asserted, and — the field a caller must not skip
// — which signatures went unchecked.
//
// On failure the refusal names the certificate and the reason:
// `X509CertExpired(index: 1, ...)`, `X509NotACertificateAuthority(index:
// 2)`, `X509UnknownCriticalExtension(index: 0, oid: "...")`.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use civil
// use asn1read
// use x509cert
// use x509path
// use x509verify
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let der = bytes.zeros(0)
//     let at = civil.datetime(civil.date_from_epoch_day(20713), civil.midnight())
//     match x509cert.parse_all(der, asn1read.default_limits())
//         Err(e)    => println(e.message())
//         Ok(chain) =>
//             match x509path.validate(der, chain, x509path.empty_trust_set(),
//                                     x509verify.no_verifier(), at,
//                                     x509path.strict())
//                 Ok(p)  => println("${p.length}")
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn validate(src: Bytes, chain: [X509Cert], trust: X509TrustSet, verifier: X509Verifier,
                at: CivilDateTime, policy: X509PathPolicy) -> Result<X509Path, X509Error> []
    let unusable = check_policy(policy)
    if let Some(e) = unusable
        return Err(e)
    if list.len(chain) == 0
        return Err(X509EmptyChain)
    if anchor_count(trust) == 0
        return Err(X509EmptyTrustSet)
    if list.len(chain) > policy.max_chain_length
        return Err(X509ChainTooLong(length: list.len(chain),
                                    limit: policy.max_chain_length))
    let top_index = list.len(chain) - 1
    let top_issuer = x509cert.issuer_of(chain[top_index])
    let Some(anchor) = find_anchor(trust, src, top_issuer) else
        return Err(X509IssuerNotFound(index: top_index,
                                      issuer: x509name.to_rfc4514(top_issuer)))
    // No limits argument on this call: a caller validating certificates
    // it already holds passes the bytes, not the budget they were read
    // under, and the default is the budget this package parses with.
    let limits = asn1read.default_limits()
    var unchecked: [Int] = []
    var policies: [AsnOid] = []
    var intermediates = 0
    for i in 0..list.len(chain)
        let c = chain[i]
        if i > 0 and not x509cert.is_self_issued(src, c)
            intermediates = intermediates + 1
        // `is_issuer` is about THIS certificate, and in a chain written
        // end-entity first certificate `i` is the issuer of certificate
        // `i - 1`: everything above the end-entity certificate signed
        // something, and the end-entity certificate signed nothing.  It
        // is `i > 0` and not "not the last", because demanding the CA
        // flag of the leaf refuses every ordinary chain of two
        // certificates — the leaf is where a chain starts and never an
        // issuer.
        let own = check_certificate(src, c, i, i > 0, at, policy, limits)
        if let Some(e) = own
            return Err(e)
        let asserted = x509ext.certificate_policies(c.extensions, limits)!
        if let Some(asserting) = asserted
            for p in asserting
                list.push(policies, asn1oid.oid(asn1oid.arcs_of(p.oid)))
        let step = if i < top_index
            link(src, c, src, chain[i + 1], i, intermediates, verifier, policy, limits, false)
        else
            link(src, c, anchor.der, anchor.cert, i, intermediates, verifier, policy, limits, true)
        if let Some(e) = step.fault
            return Err(e)
        if step.unchecked
            list.push(unchecked, i)
        if policy.enforce_name_constraints
            let above = constraints_above(src, chain, i, anchor, limits)!
            let names = asserted_names(c, limits)!
            let refused = first_refused(above, names)
            if let Some(n) = refused
                return Err(X509NameConstraintViolated(index: i,
                                                      name: x509ext.general_name_text(n)))
    Ok(X509Path { certificates: list.clone(chain), anchor: anchor, unchecked: unchecked,
                  policies: policies, length: list.len(chain) })

// Parse a chain out of one buffer and validate it, in one call.
//
// The convenience over `x509cert.parse_all` and `validate` for the
// ordinary case: a TLS Certificate message, or a `fullchain` file with
// the base64 already removed.  Every argument means what it means
// above.
pub fn validate_der(src: Bytes, trust: X509TrustSet, verifier: X509Verifier, at: CivilDateTime,
                    policy: X509PathPolicy, limits: AsnLimits) -> Result<X509Path, X509Error> []
    let chain = x509cert.parse_all(src, limits)!
    validate(src, chain, trust, verifier, at, policy)

// Put a chain in order: the leaf first, then whichever certificate
// issued it, up to one an anchor issued.
//
// For a caller handed a bag of certificates in no order, which is what
// a PKCS #7 bundle is and what a badly configured server sends.  The
// issuer of each is found by comparing name ENCODINGS, and
// `X509IssuerNotFound` names the certificate whose issuer is missing.
//
// It builds ONE chain.  Where two certificates could both be the
// issuer — a cross-signed CA, which is how a root is retired — the
// first that matches wins, and a caller that needs both tried needs to
// try both itself.  Saying so is better than a builder that
// backtracks in ways nobody can predict from a log line.
pub fn build_chain(src: Bytes, leaf: X509Cert, pool: [X509Cert],
                   trust: X509TrustSet) -> Result<[X509Cert], X509Error> []
    var out: [X509Cert] = [leaf]
    loop
        let top = out[list.len(out) - 1]
        let issuer = x509cert.issuer_of(top)
        var next: ?X509Cert = None
        for c in pool
            if x509name.issuer_matches_subject(src, issuer, src, x509cert.subject_of(c))
                if not holds_cert(out, c)
                    if next == None
                        next = Some(c)
        if let Some(c) = next
            list.push(out, c)
        else
            let Some(_) = find_anchor(trust, src, issuer) else
                return Err(X509IssuerNotFound(index: list.len(out) - 1,
                                              issuer: x509name.to_rfc4514(issuer)))
            break
    Ok(out)

// Whether each certificate in the chain was issued by the next, by
// name — or the first refusal if one was not.
//
// Names only: no signature, no dates, no extensions.  What a caller
// checks before spending anything on a chain that is in the wrong
// order, and what a server checks about the bundle it is configured
// with.
pub fn check_chain_order(src: Bytes, chain: [X509Cert]) -> ?X509Error []
    for i in 0..list.len(chain)
        if i + 1 < list.len(chain)
            let here = x509cert.issuer_of(chain[i])
            if not x509name.issuer_matches_subject(src, here, src,
                                                   x509cert.subject_of(chain[i + 1]))
                return Some(X509IssuerNotFound(index: i,
                                               issuer: x509name.to_rfc4514(here)))
    None

// The refusal a certificate's own rules produce at this instant under
// this policy, or `None`.
//
// One certificate, one index, no chain: validity, unknown critical
// extensions, the CA flag and the key usage if the policy requires
// them.  Published because a caller sometimes has one certificate and
// a question about it, and because `validate` is built out of it.
pub fn check_certificate(src: Bytes, c: X509Cert, index: Int, is_issuer: Bool, at: CivilDateTime,
                         policy: X509PathPolicy, limits: AsnLimits) -> ?X509Error []
    if policy.check_validity
        let stale = x509cert.check_validity_at(c, at, index)
        if let Some(e) = stale
            return Some(e)
    let unknown = x509ext.check_unknown_critical(x509cert.extensions_of(c), index,
                                                 policy.known_extension_oids)
    if let Some(e) = unknown
        return Some(e)
    if let Some(purpose) = policy.required_key_purpose
        match x509ext.extended_key_usage(x509cert.extensions_of(c), limits)
            Err(e)       =>
                return Some(e)
            Ok(purposes) =>
                if let Some(ps) = purposes
                    if not x509ext.has_key_purpose(ps, purpose)
                        return Some(X509KeyPurposeNotPermitted(index: index,
                                        purpose: asn1oid.oid_text(purpose)))
    if is_issuer and policy.require_ca_flag
        match x509ext.is_ca(x509cert.extensions_of(c), limits)
            Err(e) =>
                return Some(e)
            Ok(ca) =>
                if not ca
                    return Some(X509NotACertificateAuthority(index: index))
    if is_issuer and policy.require_key_cert_sign
        match x509ext.key_usage(x509cert.extensions_of(c), limits)
            Err(e)    =>
                return Some(e)
            Ok(usage) =>
                if let Some(k) = usage
                    if not k.key_cert_sign
                        return Some(X509KeyCertSignNotPermitted(index: index))
    None

// ── reading the answer ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// The end-entity certificate the path was built for.
pub fn leaf_of(p: X509Path) -> X509Cert []
    p.certificates[0]

// The anchor the path terminated at.
pub fn anchor_of(p: X509Path) -> X509Anchor []
    p.anchor

// How many certificates sit below the anchor.
pub fn path_length(p: X509Path) -> Int []
    p.length

// The index of every certificate whose signature went unchecked.
//
// Empty when every one was checked.
pub fn unchecked_of(p: X509Path) -> [Int] []
    p.unchecked[:]

// Whether every signature on the path was actually checked.
//
// The one question a caller must ask before it says "valid" out loud.
//
// ```novo
// use x509path
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${x509path.strict().require_all_signatures_checked}")   // true
// ```
pub fn is_fully_checked(p: X509Path) -> Bool []
    list.len(p.unchecked) == 0

// Every policy OID asserted anywhere on the path.
pub fn policies_of(p: X509Path) -> [AsnOid] []
    p.policies[:]

// ── names ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Whether a certificate's names match a host, by RFC 6125 section
// 6.4.
//
// The subject alternative names are matched and the common name is
// NOT: RFC 6125 deprecated that in 2011 and RFC 9525 removed it in
// 2024.  A wildcard matches one label, in the leftmost position only,
// so `*.example.com` matches `a.example.com` and neither
// `example.com` nor `a.b.example.com`.
//
// It is here rather than in a TLS package because the rule is about
// what a certificate asserts, and because a caller checking a chain
// and then forgetting to check the name has done the expensive half
// and skipped the one that matters.
pub fn matches_hostname(names: [X509GeneralName], host: Str) -> Bool []
    let wanted = str.lower(host)
    for n in names
        if let X509DnsName(name) = n
            if dns_matches(str.lower(name), wanted)
                return true
    false

// Whether a certificate's names fall inside every name constraint on
// the path above it.
//
// The check `enforce_name_constraints` turns on, published on its own
// so a caller can ask it about a certificate it is considering issuing
// under a constrained CA.
pub fn names_permitted(constraints: [X509NameConstraints], names: [X509GeneralName]) -> Bool []
    match first_refused(constraints, names)
        Some(_) => false
        None    => true
